# Browser Emulator Mandatory Workflow

When working in `cursor/tool/browser-emulator/`, follow this order:

0. **Review device first.** If `output/review-mode.local.json` is missing, ask the human: this computer (desktop) or another device (hands-free). Save with `node emulator.js review-mode --mode desktop|hands-free`. Do not start hold until that is saved. Another-device feedback **requires** hands-free.
1. Read `cursor/tool/browser-emulator/TESTING_RULES.md` before running tests or making changes.
2. Read domain flow note first: `cursor/tool/browser-emulator/flow/domain/<domain>.md` (fallback `flow/domain/default.md`).
3. If a request includes run/test/login/navigation, use the checklist from `TESTING_RULES.md` first.
4. Prefer running known scenarios from committed configs. For ad-hoc flows, do not create `test/tmp/*.json` scripts.
5. Never place real credentials in tracked files. Use local-only files (ignored by git).
6. In test reports, include: tested URL, runTag, status, finalUrl, and artifact paths.
7. For exploratory flows, use iterative pattern: think -> one action -> save with `--saveCaseNoteTo` + `--caseKey` -> continue.
8. In live mode, keep browser session reusable between commands and only close when user explicitly asks.
9. After each run, read `output/runs/<runTag>/agent-state.json` first, then read the latest screenshot image artifact, then choose next action.
10. For case runs, read latest `output/runs/<runTag>/steps/` captures before deciding next step.
11. For each step, read `steps/step-<n>-review.json` and use its `nextSuggestion` before running the next action.
12. Keep `reviewBeforeNextStep` enabled to enforce review gate; do not continue blindly.
13. For case runs, one step runs per command by default; always review artifacts before continuing.
14. Default to Edge CDP attach flow (`useCdp: true`) unless user explicitly asks for local launch (Edge is best/recommended).
15. For attach matching, prefer existing tab on same target domain before opening a new tab.
16. Ensure `output/last-domain-flow.json` is reviewed after run when selector issues occur.
17. Ensure `output/last-domain-page-summary.json` is reviewed before and after runs to keep route memory fresh.
18. For all agent `node emulator.js ...` commands, enforce timeout range: min `1000` ms, max `8000` ms.
19. Prefer explicit timeout on each command (`--timeout 3000` recommended).
20. Do not use `--forceLocalLaunch true` unless user explicitly requests local launch.
21. For CDP auto-start, always set startup retry policy (`--cdpStartupRetries 2` minimum).
22. If first CDP startup fails, retry with detached launch (`--cdpRetryLaunchDetached true`) unless user requires foreground only.
23. For non-interactive runs in any mode, append `--background true`.
24. Use mandatory auto-retry loop on failures: read `agent-state.json` + step review + failed artifacts, and explicitly read the failed screenshot image file before deciding next retry.
25. Do not report success unless the requested selector/behavior is visible in captured DOM/screenshot.
26. For selector failures, escalate in order: stable selector -> role/text -> scoped selector -> explicit open-panel step.
27. Do not clear session/cache by default for logged-in workspace tests: keep `forgetPageAfterRun=false`, `forgetDomainCacheAfterRun=false`, `forgetSessionAfterRun=false` unless user explicitly asks for privacy wipe.
28. If failure appears caused by emulator/tool behavior (artifact path bug, exit code bug, capture bug, attach bug), diagnose with artifacts first and retry with usage-level adjustments.
29. Do NOT auto-edit browser-emulator source/rules/docs from this rule flow; tool code changes require explicit user request.
30. Retry loop is required without waiting for extra user prompt; NEVER stop immediately after a failed run.
31. On failure, run at least 3 focused retries (new `runTag` each time) unless PASS happens earlier.
32. Escalate to HARD BLOCKER only after retry attempts are exhausted and evidence is attached.
33. Screenshot review is required on each cycle (success and failure); do not skip image review and rely only on text logs.

## Mandatory hold-for-user-answer after UI tasks

## Mandatory hold after UI tasks

Read `output/review-mode.local.json` (or ask + save) before hold.

If mode is **hands-free** (another device):

1. `node emulator.js hands-free --origin http://127.0.0.1 --hostHeader <vhost> --holdTimeoutMs 600000 --runTag hands-free-1`
2. Block the Shell call. Read `HANDS_FREE_URL` and `HANDS_FREE_HOLD_RECEIVED`.
3. After submit, read `output/runs/<runTag>/hold-answer.json` plus hold images.
4. After edits, `hands-free-reload --state listening`, then wait again with `hands-free`.
5. `hands-free-stop` when the other-device session ends.
6. NEVER use CDP `holdForUserAnswer` while hands-free is saved.
7. Chrome blocks tunnel pages from loading loopback. Restart `hands-free` if the other device still requests `127.0.0.1` or other local origins. Pass `--extraOrigin` for API hosts; do not guess names from the app vhost.

If mode is **desktop** (this computer), the agent MUST:

1. Navigate the browser to the exact edited zone (open the correct page, click the right panel/tab).
2. Run `node emulator.js action --type holdForUserAnswer` with:
   - `--selector` pointing at the edited element
   - `--prompt` describing the change and asking user to annotate/approve
   - `--holdTimeoutMs 600000`
   - hold duration must come from `--holdTimeoutMs`, not generic `--timeout`
   - include `--acceptLabel` so user can explicitly confirm done from toolbar
3. The Shell call MUST use `block_until_ms: 660000` (11 min) so the agent blocks and waits.
   NEVER use `block_until_ms: 0` for holdForUserAnswer — that backgrounds it and the agent loses the wait.
4. Hold is considered complete only when one of these happens:
   - user clicks toolbar accept
   - user submits feedback from toolbar
   - hold timeout is reached
   Do NOT treat generic CLI success text as hold completion.
5. After user submits, immediately read and cite the artifacts:
   - `output/runs/<runTag>/hold-answer.json`
   - `output/runs/<runTag>/hold-annotation-*.png`
   - `output/runs/<runTag>/hold-composite-*.png`
6. Use the submitted answer to decide next steps automatically (no extra user prompt):
   - `decision=accept`: report done
   - `decision=feedback`: fix and re-hold
7. Loop is mandatory: continue hold/fix/retest until timeout or explicit accept. Never stop early.
8. For every `decision=feedback` cycle, this sequence is mandatory:
   - apply focused fix(es)
   - reopen the exact edited zone in browser
   - run holdForUserAnswer again
   - read artifacts again
   - only then decide next step

Hard rules:

- This is NOT optional. Every UI task must end with hold mode.
- NEVER declare UI task done without hold-for-user-answer.
- NEVER skip navigating to the edited zone first.
- NEVER background the hold command. It must block the agent turn.
- NEVER guess user satisfaction; wait for explicit submission.
- NEVER stop hold mode while user is still typing.
- NEVER rely on generic `--timeout` to control hold duration.
- NEVER stop after reading hold submission; agent must think and continue automatically.
- NEVER stop while decision is feedback; continue until accept or timeout/hard blocker.
- NEVER do multiple edit cycles in a row without reopening zone + hold again.

Before any `node emulator.js ...` command, verify:

- target URL is correct
- runTag is set
- privacy/forget settings match the task
- for normal logged-in workspace flows, enforce no-forget session policy to avoid logout
- command includes Edge attach flags (`--useCdp true`, `--cdpEndpoint "http://127.0.0.1:9223"`) unless user requested local launch
- command includes CDP startup retry flags for first-time reliability (`--cdpAutoStart true`, `--cdpStartupRetries 2`, `--cdpRetryLaunchDetached true`)
- timeout is within `1000..8000` ms
- if retrying, change only one variable per rerun and document the delta

